A glyph run draws with the font face that DirectWrite shaped it with. Before, a face that the text system did not
create (often a fallback face) went through its family, weight and style to a font that was selected again. That
can be a different face: for an italic run, DirectWrite gives an upright fallback face with simulated oblique, and
the new selection took another face of the family (Segoe UI Italic for Segoe UI with simulated oblique). The run
then drew its glyph ids from the wrong face, so italic Arabic, Hebrew, Japanese and Thai text showed wrong glyphs.
Now the run's own face, with its simulations, becomes the font, or a font that holds an equal face reference is
used again. Only the faces that the text system keeps are keys of the face cache, so the address of a released
face never names a font. `an_italic_fallback_run_draws_the_glyphs_of_the_face_that_shaped_it` lays out Hebrew,
Japanese and Thai in Segoe UI Italic and checks each glyph id against the cmap of its run's face. Our own.

